\label{lem:converse}
For the $({\mathsf K},{\mathsf U}, {\mathsf S})$ information theoretic secure aggregation problem with uncoded groupwise keys, any achievable rate tuple $({\mathsf R}_1,{\mathsf R}_2)$ satisfies  
\begin{align}
{\mathsf R}_1 \geq 1, \ {\mathsf R}_2 \geq \frac{1}{{\mathsf U}}. \label{eq:converse from litterature} 
\end{align}
\hfill $\square$

\label{thm:subregion}
For the $({\mathsf K},{\mathsf U}, {\mathsf S})$ information theoretic secure aggregation problem with uncoded groupwise keys, when $1={\mathsf S} \leq  {\mathsf K}-{\mathsf U}$, secure aggregation is not possible; when  $2\leq {\mathsf S} \leq  {\mathsf K}-{\mathsf U}$,  any achievable rate tuple $({\mathsf R}_1,{\mathsf R}_2)$ satisfies  
\begin{align}
{\mathsf R}_1\geq  1+\frac{1}{  \binom{{\mathsf K}-1}{{\mathsf S}-1}-1}, {\mathsf R}_2 \geq \frac{1}{{\mathsf U}} .\label{eq:converse of R1}
\end{align} 
\hfill $\square$

\label{thm:ach result}
For the $({\mathsf K},{\mathsf U}, {\mathsf S})$ information theoretic secure aggregation problem with uncoded groupwise keys, when ${\mathsf S} > {\mathsf K}-{\mathsf U}$, the following rate tuples are achievable, 
\begin{align}
{\mathsf R}_1 \geq 1, {\mathsf R}_2\geq \frac{1}{{\mathsf U}} . \label{eq:S>K-U result thm}
\end{align}
\hfill $\square$

\label{thm:main result}
For the $({\mathsf K},{\mathsf U}, {\mathsf S})$ information theoretic secure aggregation problem with uncoded groupwise keys,  when ${\mathsf S}=1$, secure aggregation is not possible; when  ${\mathsf S}\geq 2$, we have 
\begin{align}
{\mathcal R}^{\star} = \left\{({\mathsf R}_1,{\mathsf R}_2): {\mathsf R}_1 \geq \frac{\binom{{\mathsf K}-1}{{\mathsf S}-1}}{\binom{{\mathsf K}-1}{{\mathsf S}-1}-\binom{{\mathsf K}-1-{\mathsf U}}{{\mathsf S}-1}}, {\mathsf R}_2\geq \frac{1}{{\mathsf U}} \right\}. \label{eq:main result thm}
\end{align}
\hfill $\square$

\label{ex:case 1}
\rm
We consider the   $({\mathsf K},{\mathsf U}, {\mathsf S})=(5,2,3)$ information theoretic secure aggregation problem with uncoded groupwise keys. Note that in this example,  for the ease of illustration, we assume that 
the field size ${\mathsf q}$ is a large enough prime; it will be proved later that our proposed scheme works for arbitrary field size. 

 By the converse bound derived in Section~\ref{sec:converse bound}, we have 
$
{\mathsf R}_1\geq \frac{\binom{{\mathsf K}-1}{{\mathsf S}-1}}{\binom{{\mathsf K}-1}{{\mathsf S}-1}-\binom{{\mathsf K}-1-{\mathsf U}}{{\mathsf S}-1}} = \frac{6}{5}
$
and ${\mathsf R}_2\geq \frac{1}{{\mathsf U}}=\frac{1}{2}$. 
 Inspired by the converse bound, we divide each input vector $W_i$ where $i\in [{\mathsf K}]$ into $\binom{{\mathsf K}-1}{{\mathsf S}-1}-\binom{{\mathsf K}-1-{\mathsf U}}{{\mathsf S}-1}=5$ non-overlapping and equal-length pieces, $W_{i}=\{W_{i,1},\ldots,W_{i,5}\}$. 
For each set ${\mathcal V}\in \binom{[{\mathsf K}]}{{\mathsf S}}$, we generate a key $Z_{{\mathcal V}}$ containing $\frac{{\mathsf S}{\mathsf L}}{\binom{{\mathsf K}-1}{{\mathsf S}-1}-\binom{{\mathsf K}-1-{\mathsf U}}{{\mathsf S}-1}}=\frac{3{\mathsf L}}{5}$ symbols uniformly i.i.d. over $\mathbb{F}_{{\mathsf q}}$; let $Z_{{\mathcal V}}$ be shared by the users in ${\mathcal V}$.  In addition, we further divide each key $Z_{{\mathcal V}}$ into ${\mathsf S}=3$ sub-keys, $Z_{{\mathcal V}}=\{Z_{{\mathcal V},k}:k\in {\mathcal V}\}$, where each sub-key $Z_{{\mathcal V},k}$ has $\frac{{\mathsf L}}{5}$ symbols. 

From the converse bound we see that in the first round each user $k\in [5]$ should send more than   ${\mathsf L}$ symbols, 

 while input vector $W_k$ contains ${\mathsf L}$ symbols.
Thus, unlike the secure aggregation scheme in~\cite{groupwisekey2022wan} which has ${\mathsf R}_1=1$, in the first round besides the encrypted input vector, we also need to transmit some coded messages composed of keys, to cope with the fact that   some keys cannot be transmitted in the second round due to   user dropouts.
For each key ${\mathcal Z}_{{\mathcal V}}$, we select a $6$-length vector ${\mathbf a}_{{\mathcal V}}=[a_{{\mathcal V},1},\ldots,a_{{\mathcal V},6}]^{\text{\rm T}}$ which will serve as the coefficient vector of its sub-keys during the first round. 
The selection of these coefficient vectors to guarantee the encodability, decodability and security, is the most important step in the proposed secure aggregation scheme. 
We  denote  the sets ${\mathcal V}\in \binom{[{\mathsf K}]}{{\mathsf S}}$ where   $k \in {\mathcal V}$  by  ${\mathcal S}_{k,1},\ldots,{\mathcal S}_{k,\binom{{\mathsf K}-1}{{\mathsf S}-1}}$; denote 
the sets  in $\binom{[{\mathsf K}]\setminus\{k\}}{{\mathsf S}}$   by  
$\overline{{\mathcal S}}_{k,1},\ldots,\overline{{\mathcal S}}_{k,\binom{{\mathsf K}-1}{{\mathsf S}}}$.
 For the security and encodability, it will be explained later that the selection   has the following two properties respectively:
\begin{align}
& \left[{\mathbf a}_{{\mathcal S}_{k,1}}, \ldots, {\mathbf a}_{{\mathcal S}_{k,\binom{{\mathsf K}-1}{{\mathsf S}-1}}} \right] \ \text{ has rank equal to } \binom{{\mathsf K}-1}{{\mathsf S}-1}=6, \ \forall k\in [{\mathsf K}]; \label{eq:property example security}\\ 
& \left[{\mathbf a}_{\overline{{\mathcal S}}_{k,1}}, \ldots, {\mathbf a}_{\overline{{\mathcal S}}_{k,\binom{{\mathsf K}-1}{{\mathsf S}}}}  \right] \ \text{ has rank equal to } \binom{{\mathsf K}-2}{{\mathsf S}-1}=3, \ \forall k\in [{\mathsf K}]. \label{eq:property example encodability}
\end{align}
In order to guarantee~\eqref{eq:property example security} and~\eqref{eq:property example encodability}, we select the coefficient vectors by the following two steps:
\begin{itemize}
\item We first select each vector  ${\mathbf a}_{{\mathcal V}}$ for each ${\mathcal V} \in \binom{[{\mathsf K}]}{{\mathsf S}}$ where $1\in {\mathcal V}$. More precisely, we choose each element in  ${\mathbf a}_{{\mathcal V}}$ uniformly i.i.d. over $\mathbb{F}_{{\mathsf q}}$.  
 In this example, we let 
\begin{align*}
& {\mathbf a}_{\{1,2,3\}}=[0, 1, 0, 0, 1, 1]^{\text{\rm T}}, \ {\mathbf a}_{\{1,2,4\}}=[1, 0, 1, 1, 1, 1]^{\text{\rm T}},\ {\mathbf a}_{\{1,2,5\}}=[0, 0, 0, 1, 0, 1]^{\text{\rm T}},\\
& {\mathbf a}_{\{1,3,4\}}=[0, 1, 1, 1, 0, 1]^{\text{\rm T}},\ {\mathbf a}_{\{1,3,5\}}=[1, 1, 0, 1, 0, 1]^{\text{\rm T}},\ {\mathbf a}_{\{1,4,5\}}=[1, 0, 0, 0, 0, 1]^{\text{\rm T}}. 
\end{align*}
\item Then we fix each of the remaining vectors  by a linear combination  of the selected vectors in the first step. More precisely, to fix ${\mathbf a}_{\{2,3,4\}}$, 
we let ${\mathbf a}_{\{2,3,4\}}$ be a linear combination  of  ${\mathbf a}_{\{1,3,4\}}$, ${\mathbf a}_{\{1,2,4\}}$, and ${\mathbf a}_{\{1,2,3\}}$, where the coefficients are either $+1$ or $-1$ and   alternated,
\begin{align}
{\mathbf a}_{\{2,3,4\}}= {\mathbf a}_{\{1,3,4\}}- {\mathbf a}_{\{1,2,4\}} +{\mathbf a}_{\{1,2,3\}}.\label{eq:ex1 av234}
\end{align}
Similarly, for each ${\mathcal V} \in \binom{[2:{\mathsf K}]}{{\mathsf S}}$,  we let ${\mathbf a}_{{\mathcal V}}$ be the following linear combination of ${\mathbf a}_{{\mathcal V}\setminus\{k\} \cup \{1\}}$ where $k\in {\mathcal V}$, (recall that ${\mathcal V}(i)$ represents the $i^{\text{th}}$ smallest element in ${\mathcal V}$) 
\begin{align}
{\mathbf a}_{{\mathcal V}}= \sum_{i\in [3]} (-1)^{i-1} {\mathbf a}_{{\mathcal V}\setminus \{{\mathcal V}(i)\} \cup \{1\}}.\label{eq:ex1 av}
\end{align}
\end{itemize}
The detailed section on the coefficient vectors is given in Table~\ref{tab:ex1}. It can be checked that this selection has the two properties in~\eqref{eq:property example security} and~\eqref{eq:property example encodability}. 
The first property could be directly checked. For the second property, we have  
\begin{align}
{\mathbf a}_{\{3,4,5\}}= {\mathbf a}_{\{2,4,5\}}-{\mathbf a}_{\{2,3,5\}} +{\mathbf a}_{\{2,3,4\}}; \label{eq:av 345 for user 1}
\end{align}
thus the rank of $[{\mathbf a}_{\{2,3,4\}},{\mathbf a}_{\{2,3,5\}},{\mathbf a}_{\{2,4,5\}},{\mathbf a}_{\{3,4,5\}}]$ is equal to the rank of $[{\mathbf a}_{\{2,3,4\}},{\mathbf a}_{\{2,3,5\}},{\mathbf a}_{\{2,4,5\}}]$ which is equal to $3$. In addition, since 
\begin{align}
{\mathbf a}_{\{3,4,5\}}= {\mathbf a}_{\{1,4,5\}}-{\mathbf a}_{\{1,3,5\}} +{\mathbf a}_{\{1,3,4\}};\label{eq:av 345 for user 2}
\end{align}
thus the rank of $[{\mathbf a}_{\{1,3,4\}},{\mathbf a}_{\{1,3,5\}},{\mathbf a}_{\{1,4,5\}},{\mathbf a}_{\{3,4,5\}}]$ is equal to the rank of $[{\mathbf a}_{\{1,3,4\}},{\mathbf a}_{\{1,3,5\}},{\mathbf a}_{\{1,4,5\}}]$ which is equal to $3$. Similarly, we can also check that 
the rank of  $[{\mathbf a}_{\{1,2,4\}},{\mathbf a}_{\{1,2,5\}},{\mathbf a}_{\{1,4,5\}},{\mathbf a}_{\{2,4,5\}}]$ is equal to the rank of $[{\mathbf a}_{\{1,2,4\}},{\mathbf a}_{\{1,2,5\}},{\mathbf a}_{\{1,4,5\}}]$ which is equal to $3$; the rank of  $[{\mathbf a}_{\{1,2,3\}},{\mathbf a}_{\{1,2,5\}},{\mathbf a}_{\{1,3,5\}},{\mathbf a}_{\{2,3,5\}}]$ is equal to the rank of $[{\mathbf a}_{\{1,2,3\}},{\mathbf a}_{\{1,2,5\}},{\mathbf a}_{\{1,3,5\}}]$ which is equal to $3$; the rank of  $[{\mathbf a}_{\{1,2,3\}},{\mathbf a}_{\{1,2,4\}},{\mathbf a}_{\{1,3,4\}},{\mathbf a}_{\{2,3,4\}}]$ is equal to the rank of $[{\mathbf a}_{\{1,2,3\}},{\mathbf a}_{\{1,2,4\}},{\mathbf a}_{\{1,3,4\}}]$ which is equal to $3$. Thus the property is satisfied. 
We will show later that this selection     guarantees the encodability, decodability and security.

\begin{table*}
 \centering
\protect\caption{Choice of $6$-dimensional vectors ${\mathbf a}_{{\mathcal V}}$ in the  $({\mathsf K},{\mathsf U}, {\mathsf S}))=(5,2,3)$ information theoretic secure aggregation problem. }\label{tab:ex1}
\begin{tabular}{|c|c|c|c|}
\hline 
${\mathbf a}_{{\mathcal V}}$   & Value & ${\mathbf a}_{{\mathcal V}}$   & Value 
 \tabularnewline  
\hline 


\hline 
  ${\mathbf a}_{\{1,2,3\}} $  &  $[0, 1, 0, 0, 1, 1]^{\text{\rm T}}$   &   ${\mathbf a}_{\{1,4,5\}} $    & $[1, 0, 0, 0, 0, 1]^{\text{\rm T}}$ \tabularnewline  
\hline 
  ${\mathbf a}_{\{1,2,4\}} $  &  $[1, 0, 1, 1, 1, 1]^{\text{\rm T}}$    &   ${\mathbf a}_{\{2,3,4\}} $    & $ {\mathbf a}_{\{1,3,4\}}- {\mathbf a}_{\{1,2,4\}} +{\mathbf a}_{\{1,2,3\}}=[-1, 2, 0, 0, 0, 1]^{\text{\rm T}}$  \tabularnewline  
